data: {"step": "response", "status": "streaming", "detail": "The current caseload reveals several critical anomalies and trends warranting immediate attention:  \n\n1. **Severe record incompleteness** undermines case tracking and timelines: 9 FIRs lack incident dates entirely [Document 1], and 52 FIRs have no recorded investigation status, leaving their progress undetectable and risking stagnation.  \n\n2. **Repeat offenders** appear across multiple cases, suggesting potential ongoing criminal networks:  \n   - **\u0641\u06cc\u0635\u0644** and **\u0637\u0627\u0631\u0642** both appear in **fir-202-26** and **fir-401-26** [Document 2].  \n   - **\u0634\u06c1\u0632\u06cc\u0628 \u0639\u0631\u0641 \u0634\u0627\u0628\u06cc** is linked to **fir-214-26** and **fir-891-24** [Document 2].  \n   - **\u0639\u0627\u0635\u0645 \u0631\u0634\u06cc\u062f** is tied to **fir-64-26** and **fir-65-26** [Document 2].  \n\n3. **Weapon licensing violations** are rampant: 30 recovered weapons are explicitly recorded without licences [Document 3], and 2 entries lack licence status entirely, leaving compliance unverifiable.  \n\n4. **Case-type diversification** since 2024 indicates shifting priorities or emerging crime patterns:  \n   - **PPC cases** have surged from 13 to 39 [Document 4].  \n   - New categories like **CNSA 1997** (12 cases) and **Punjab Domestic Violence Act** (4 cases) now feature prominently [Document 4].  \n\n5. **Court outcome discrepancies** are extreme: only 1 of 33 criminal records matches its court outcome [Document 5], raising concerns about data integrity or procedural errors.  \n\nThese findings highlight systemic gaps in record-keeping, potential repeat offender networks, widespread weapon law violations, and a legal landscape evolving to address domestic and economic crimes.\n\n_The natural-language summary could not be verified as an accurate paraphrase; showing the raw computed aggregate instead._"}

